  "summary": "Recent preprints and a 2026 publication extend constrained metabolic models to multi-medium gap-filling, allocation costs, and genome-based prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The three items share a modeling strategy in which metabolic behaviour is derived from explicit constraints or mechanistic representations rather than specified directly. The arXiv study treats gap-filling of genome-scale metabolic models as a combinatorial optimisation problem, solved with a metaheuristic across many media conditions. The bioRxiv study applies nonlinear growth balance analysis to coarse-grained microbial models to quantify growth costs of suboptimal protein allocation, including enzyme saturation. The BMC Bioinformatics study uses a probabilistic framework on bacterial genomes to predict indole-3-acetic acid synthesis potential across four tryptophan-dependent pathways. What is distinct is the target: network completion, resource allocation physiology, and genotype-to-phenotype prediction of a specific metabolite, rather than a shared method or a common hypothesis tested across the set.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
